      { "text": "Surgical thoracotomy with open lung biopsy.", "explanation": "Incorrect. Surgical lung biopsy is invasive and risky; bronchoscopy with BAL is much less invasive and adequate for diagnosis. This would be the correct answer if BAL had been non-diagnostic and the patient had a focal lesion accessible to surgery." },
      { "text": "Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) and possibly transbronchial biopsy", "explanation": "Correct. The clinical picture (advanced AIDS, CD4 4, subacute respiratory illness, bilateral ground-glass opacities without effusion, profound hypoxia despite quiet exam) is the classic PCP presentation. Bronchoscopy with BAL is the appropriate diagnostic procedure: send the lavage fluid for methenamine silver staining (the gold standard for Pneumocystis cyst identification), PCR/antigen testing of BAL, bacterial/fungal/mycobacterial cultures, respiratory viral panel, and CMV PCR. β-D-glucan in serum is also useful (often elevated but can be negative as in 'Bobby's case'). Pathology should show foamy macrophages — a buzzword. While awaiting confirmation, empiric high-dose IV TMP/SMX is initiated with adjunctive prednisone (severe disease with PaO2 <70 mmHg or A-a gradient ≥35 benefits from steroids). Differential includes other AIDS pulmonary opportunistic infections (TB, MAC, CMV pneumonitis, Cryptococcus, histoplasmosis), so broad workup is needed alongside empiric treatment." },
      { "text": "Routine sputum culture without bronchoscopy.", "explanation": "Incorrect. Sputum culture is less sensitive than BAL for Pneumocystis, especially in critically ill patients who cannot expectorate effectively. This would be the correct answer in less severe disease where induced sputum could be obtained." },
      { "text": "CT angiogram of the pulmonary arteries to rule out PE.", "explanation": "Incorrect. While PE can be in the differential, the clinical and imaging picture strongly favors PCP, and CT angiogram does not establish the PCP diagnosis. This would be the correct answer if PE were the leading concern (e.g., unilateral leg swelling, normal CXR)." }
