    "category": "Microbiology: PCP Prophylaxis",
    "questionText": "A 36-year-old man has just been diagnosed with HIV. CD4 count is 165 cells/uL. He is started on antiretroviral therapy. Which prophylactic medication should also be initiated for opportunistic infection prevention?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Isoniazid 9 months for TB without testing.", "explanation": "Incorrect. INH prophylaxis (or alternative shorter regimens) is given when TB testing is positive, not empirically. This would be the correct answer in a patient with positive PPD or IGRA." },
      { "text": "Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (TMP/SMX) for Pneumocystis jirovecii prophylaxis", "explanation": "Correct. The lecturer specified that PCP prophylaxis is indicated at CD4 <200 cells/uL. This patient at CD4 165 needs PCP prophylaxis. Standard regimen is TMP/SMX (Bactrim) double-strength tablet three times weekly (Monday/Wednesday/Friday) or once daily. Alternatives for sulfa-allergic patients: dapsone (test G6PD first to avoid hemolysis), atovaquone (suspension, expensive but well-tolerated), or aerosolized pentamidine (less effective; reserved for severely sulfa-intolerant). Continue prophylaxis until CD4 is sustained >200 cells/uL for at least 6 months on effective ART. TMP/SMX prophylaxis at CD4 <200 also provides cross-coverage for cerebral toxoplasmosis (a risk at CD4 <100), so most patients do not need additional toxoplasmosis-specific prophylaxis. MAC prophylaxis (azithromycin weekly at CD4 <50) is no longer routinely given because MAC has become rare in the modern ART era." },
      { "text": "Empiric clarithromycin + ethambutol for MAC.", "explanation": "Incorrect. MAC prophylaxis is not indicated at CD4 165; it would be considered at CD4 <50 in older protocols (now generally not given). This would be the correct answer at CD4 <50 in a patient with prior MAC exposure or known risk." },
      { "text": "Daily fluconazole for esophageal candidiasis.", "explanation": "Incorrect. Routine fluconazole prophylaxis is not given in HIV; treatment is initiated when esophageal candidiasis develops. This would be the correct answer if the patient already had esophageal candidiasis." },
      { "text": "Empiric pyrimethamine + sulfadiazine for cerebral toxoplasmosis.", "explanation": "Incorrect. Empiric anti-toxoplasmosis is not indicated at CD4 165 (toxoplasmosis becomes a risk at CD4 <100), and TMP/SMX provides adequate cross-coverage. This would be the correct answer if the patient had positive Toxoplasma IgG and CD4 <100." }
    ],
