    "category": "Microbiology: Lady Windermere",
    "questionText": "A 65-year-old thin, elderly woman with no major medical illnesses presents with chronic cough and sputum production. CT chest shows tree-in-bud opacities, bronchiectasis, and small nodular infiltrates predominantly in the right middle lobe and lingula. Sputum AFB cultures grow Mycobacterium avium complex. Which clinical entity does this represent?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Lady Windermere syndrome", "explanation": "Correct. The lecturer specifically named Lady Windermere syndrome — pulmonary MAC disease occurring in thin, elderly women without obvious immune deficiency. The condition was named after the Oscar Wilde character (Lady Windermere refused to clear her throat in polite society — speculatively linked to inadequate cough leading to bronchiectasis and infection). The classic radiographic findings include tree-in-bud opacities, bronchiectasis, and nodular infiltrates predominantly in the right middle lobe and lingula (RML/LL distribution). Recently, the entity has been linked to a genetic susceptibility, with cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) mutations and other genetic factors implicated. Treatment is similar to disseminated MAC but for the pulmonary form: clarithromycin or azithromycin + ethambutol, often with rifampin or rifabutin (the 'three-drug regimen'), for prolonged courses (often >12 months and continued until sputum cultures are negative for ≥12 months). Macrolide resistance testing is essential. Some patients require lobectomy for severely affected segments." },
      { "text": "Mycobacterium tuberculosis with drug-resistant pattern.", "explanation": "Incorrect. The cultures grew MAC, not M. tuberculosis. The radiographic distribution (RML/lingula) and demographic (elderly thin woman without TB risk factors) also favor MAC. This would be the correct answer if the cultures grew M. tuberculosis." },
      { "text": "Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA).", "explanation": "Incorrect. ABPA presents in patients with asthma or cystic fibrosis with elevated IgE and central bronchiectasis, with positive Aspergillus IgE/IgG. This would be the correct answer if the patient had asthma or CF with positive Aspergillus serology." },
      { "text": "Cystic fibrosis with chronic Pseudomonas infection.", "explanation": "Incorrect. CF typically presents earlier in life and the cultures grew MAC, not Pseudomonas. Genetic testing for CF mutations could be considered (links to Lady Windermere). This would be the correct answer with positive sweat chloride test or CFTR mutations and cultures positive for Pseudomonas." },
      { "text": "Sarcoidosis.", "explanation": "Incorrect. Sarcoidosis typically shows bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy and parenchymal infiltrates, not the RML/lingula nodular pattern with positive AFB cultures for MAC. This would be the correct answer if biopsy showed non-caseating granulomas without organisms." }
    ],
