      { "text": "Acute retinal detachment from a subclinical retinal tear.", "explanation": "Incorrect. Retinal detachment produces different findings (visual field defect with shadow/curtain progression) and is not associated with cotton wool spots and flame hemorrhages on initial presentation. This would be the correct answer if the patient had visual field defects with retinal tear on exam." },
      { "text": "New primary CMV infection acquired since HIV diagnosis.", "explanation": "Incorrect. New primary CMV infection in an adult is rare and would not produce the classic retinitis pattern in this short timeframe. This is more likely IRIS-mediated unmasking of subclinical reactivation. This would be the correct answer in a misframed pathophysiology." },
      { "text": "Bacterial endophthalmitis.", "explanation": "Incorrect. Endophthalmitis features pain, vitreous opacification, and hypopyon, not the cotton wool spots + flame hemorrhages pattern of CMV retinitis. This would be the correct answer if the patient had pain and hypopyon." },
      { "text": "Unmasking IRIS", "explanation": "Correct. The clinical picture is classic 'unmasking' IRIS: CMV retinitis was likely subclinical at the time of HIV diagnosis (the immune system was too suppressed to mount an inflammatory response, so symptoms and findings were absent), and the immune recovery from ART has now allowed the inflammatory response to develop and unmask the disease. The lecturer specifically described this exact scenario — 'unmasking' IRIS occurs from undiagnosed occult infection like CMV retinitis. Classic timing: 1 week to 3-4 months after ART initiation, especially in patients starting ART with CD4 <100 (or <200 for TB). Management: continue ART (DO NOT stop — the immune recovery is therapeutic), initiate IV ganciclovir or oral valganciclovir for CMV, consider intravitreal ganciclovir injections for sight-threatening lesions, ophthalmology consultation. Corticosteroids may be added in some cases of severe IRIS-related inflammation. This scenario specifically illustrates why some experts recommend baseline ophthalmologic exam and routine fundoscopic surveillance in patients with low CD4 starting ART, and why treating known opportunistic infections for 2-4 weeks before ART initiation can reduce IRIS risk. The lecturer specified this prevention strategy explicitly: 'check for these infections and treat for at least 2-4 weeks before beginning antiretroviral therapy.'" }
